# `only` before a verb can limit the verb or anything after it (`the
# hook only fails on growth` versus `the hook fails only on growth`),
# so the reader has to pick. The technical-writing skill keeps `only`
# beside the word it limits. The rule counts `only` between a subject
# and its verb, and the filter narrows the finding to `only` and the
# verb. A progressive (`is only running`) stays out, since the tagger
# reads it as part of the verb phrase, and so does `need`, because the
# obligation rule reports `needs to`.
#
# It hit 124 lines in the kubernetes, nix.dev, and Go docs, and 10 of
# 10 sampled hits could move `only` beside the word it limits. Some
# writers mean the verb itself (`the flag only warns`), so the rule is
# a suggestion.
expr verb <VERB, !PROG, ![need, needs]>
expr main <([NOUN, PRON, PROPN, that, which, who] only @verb), (only @verb)>
let message "Only before the verb. Move only beside the word it limits."
let description "Flags only between a subject and its verb, where it can limit the verb or anything after it."
let becomes ""
let kind "Style"
allows "The hook fails only on growth."
allows "Only the owner can delete the file."
